WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.560
…is we’re going to have to automate faster.

00:02.560 --> 00:04.880
I don’t know if this automation can happen quickly enough.

00:05.320 --> 00:08.180
The meat facilities are right now running under capacity.

00:11.200 --> 00:16.700
Beef is up about 24%, depending on which article you read.
Much of it is due, you can say, because of packer control,

00:16.800 --> 00:21.420
but also because of the human hands that touch our meat in the facilities.
Can we automate fast enough?

00:21.600 --> 00:25.600
To do so, I believe that we’re going to see
more ag innovation come from outside agriculture.

00:25.600 --> 00:34.180
What if the new innovation is beyond just giving an engineer a task
and they change it 10%, but instead they change it 100%?

00:34.420 --> 00:39.540
Innovation will come to this industry from outside,
and we need to embrace it, not reject it.

00:39.760 --> 00:48.240
Because innovation that comes to agriculture might be from
a completely different, 180-degree position, and you’ll say,
“Holy crap, I can’t believe we didn’t do that.”

00:49.020 --> 00:54.980
Uber and Lyft were not founded by the CEO of Yellow Cab, were they?

00:55.600 --> 01:00.860
And I’m telling you what, I’m opposed to it.
When I was a kid at Northwest Elementary School,

01:01.300 --> 01:07.700
I remember the day that the policeman came in there in kindergarten.
The first thing he said was, “Children, don’t ever get into a car with strangers.”

01:09.560 --> 01:10.560
Now it’s a business.

01:12.320 --> 01:16.780
Think about that. You are told from the time you’re a kid,
“Don’t get in a car with a stranger.”

01:16.920 --> 01:21.480
Now it’s just to hop in a car when somebody pulls up and says,
“Are you Damian?” “No.”

01:22.740 --> 01:26.760
They can’t get your name right. I hop in a car with them.
Makes no sense to me.
